{
  "id": "a1-regular-ar-present",
  "slug": "regular-ar-present-tense",
  "title": "Regular -AR verbs in the present tense",
  "unit": "A1",
  "group": "Grammar",
  "order": 10,
  "summary": "Drop -ar and add the six endings: -o, -as, -a, -amos, -áis, -an.",
  "focus": "Present indicative conjugation of regular -ar verbs",
  "content": "## The biggest verb family\n\nMost Spanish verbs end in **-ar** (*hablar*, *trabajar*, *estudiar*, *cantar*…). Regular ones all follow the same pattern, so once you know one, you know hundreds.\n\n## The pattern\n\n1. Take the infinitive: **hablar** (to speak)\n2. Remove **-ar** to get the stem: **habl-**\n3. Add the ending for the person:\n\n| person | ending | hablar |\n|---|---|---|\n| yo | **-o** | habl**o** |\n| tú | **-as** | habl**as** |\n| él / ella / usted | **-a** | habl**a** |\n| nosotros/as | **-amos** | habl**amos** |\n| vosotros/as | **-áis** | habl**áis** |\n| ellos / ellas / ustedes | **-an** | habl**an** |\n\nNote the accent on **-áis**: *habláis*, *trabajáis*. It is the only ending with a written accent.\n\n## More examples\n\n| | trabajar (to work) | estudiar (to study) | cantar (to sing) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| yo | trabajo | estudio | canto |\n| tú | trabajas | estudias | cantas |\n| él/ella/usted | trabaja | estudia | canta |\n| nosotros/as | trabajamos | estudiamos | cantamos |\n| vosotros/as | trabajáis | estudiáis | cantáis |\n| ellos/ellas/ustedes | trabajan | estudian | cantan |\n\n## Subject pronouns are optional\n\nBecause the ending already tells you who is doing the action, Spanish usually **drops the pronoun**:\n\n- *Hablo español.* (I speak Spanish.)\n- *¿Trabajas aquí?* (Do you work here?)\n- *Estudiamos mucho.* (We study a lot.)\n\nKeep the pronoun for emphasis or contrast: *Yo trabajo, pero él no trabaja.*\n\n## Usted and ustedes\n\n*Usted* (formal *you*) uses the **él/ella** form and *ustedes* uses the **ellos/ellas** form: *¿Usted habla inglés?* In Latin America *ustedes* is used for all plural *you*, so the *vosotros* form is mainly heard in Spain.\n\n## One present, three English meanings\n\n*Canto* can mean *I sing*, *I am singing* or *I do sing*. Everyday speech has no separate simple and continuous forms to choose between.",
